# Allowlist for Dockerfile.prod's build context.
#
# This ONE file governs the context for all three stages (assets, vendor,
# runtime) - there is no per-stage .dockerignore. `*` on the first line
# excludes everything; every `!` line below re-includes exactly what one of
# the stages needs. If a stage needs something, it must be listed here even
# if the other two stages don't touch it.
#
# Why an allowlist and not a denylist: a denylist only keeps out what someone
# thought to name, so anything new in the developer's working tree (design
# review notes, IDE state, dotfiles with secrets in them, dev-only manifests)
# ships into the image by default. A missing `!` entry here instead fails
# the build loudly - composer install, npm run build, or a later COPY/chown
# in Dockerfile.prod will error on a path that isn't there - which is the
# point: the failure mode is "build breaks," not "secret ships."
#
# Docker's `!` precedence rule: the LAST matching line wins, but a `!` can
# only reach a file whose ancestor directories aren't themselves excluded by
# a bare (non-glob) directory-name pattern - `dir` blocks descending into
# `dir` even for later `!dir/keep-me` lines, whereas `dir/*` excludes dir's
# *contents* without blocking descent, so a later `!dir/keep-me` still works.
# Every exclusion below that needs a file re-included afterward therefore
# uses the `dir/*`-style (or exact-file) form, never a bare directory name.
*

# --- composer (vendor stage) + booting the framework -----------------------
# vendor stage copies the whole (filtered) context in, then runs
# `composer install`, whose post-autoload-dump hook runs
# `artisan package:discover` - which boots the framework. That needs the
# app's service providers, config, and the autoloaded database/ namespaces
# (Database\Factories, Database\Seeders - see composer.json's "autoload"),
# not just app code.
!artisan
!composer.json
!composer.lock
!app
!bootstrap
# Built from a --dev install on the host; the vendor stage regenerates them
# (and the runtime stage's COPY --from=vendor overwrites them again anyway).
bootstrap/cache/*.php
!config
!database
!routes

# --- npm (assets stage) -----------------------------------------------------
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!.npmrc
!vite.config.js
!tsconfig.json

# --- Blade views + the frontend source the assets stage compiles -----------
# One directory for both: resources/views is Blade (runtime), resources/js
# and resources/css are what `npm run build` compiles (assets stage).
!resources

# --- public/: the docroot, minus dev-only build artifacts ------------------
!public
# Generated on the host - the assets stage rebuilds it inside the image, and
# the runtime stage's COPY --from=assets overwrites this path again anyway.
public/build
# Written by `npm run dev`; in an image it would point @vite at a dead dev
# server.
public/hot
# Only written by `npm run dev` (Vite dev-server font manifest); the assets
# stage writes the production one to public/build/fonts-manifest.json.
public/fonts-manifest.dev.json

# --- deploy-time PHP-FPM config, read directly by explicit COPYs -----------
!docker/prod

# storage/ is deliberately NOT re-included here. None of its content belongs
# in the image: dev-compiled Blade views, this builder's local sessions,
# cached data, or anything a dev box's storage/app/{public,private} happened
# to hold. Dockerfile.prod's runtime stage creates the empty skeleton
# directories at build time (the same list docker/prod/entrypoint.sh
# recreates on every container start), so the app never depends on anything
# under storage/ surviving the build context - and storage/oauth/*.key (or
# any other secret one level deeper than the old `storage/*.key` denylist
# entry reached) is excluded right along with everything else under it.
